Practically:

Notice the pattern. If you've corrected the same thing twice, it's a candidate skill — not a one-off note.
Draft it narrowly. A rule plus one or two concrete examples, not a general essay on good writing.
Route it to the domain expert who owns that standard (you, a colleague, a committee) before it's trusted to run unsupervised — the same seriousness you'd give a rubric used to grade real work.
Version it. When the underlying standard changes (a new institutional policy, a revised template), the skill needs re-review, not silent drift.
Let it feed both drafting and review — the agent uses it to write, and also uses it as a checklist when checking someone else's draft against the same standard.
A skill that skips the expert-review step is just an unverified assumption with a file extension.

Two kinds of feedback: the document loop and the process loop
The Maintain stage above closes a loop per document — an outcome becomes the next intent.md. That's necessary but not sufficient. It tells you whether one policy note landed well; it doesn't tell you whether your whole workflow is still worth running the way you're running it.

Add a second, slower loop — a process reflection, on its own cadence (monthly or quarterly, not per-document):

Where did stages actually earn their keep? Did the spec step prevent a rewrite, or just add a delay? Did the review checklist catch a real problem, or has it become a rubber stamp you click past?
Where is your attention actually going? If you're still reading every draft start to finish, the layered-review shift in Stage 5 hasn't really happened yet — worth naming that honestly rather than assuming the process works because it's documented.
Are skills drifting from the standards they encode? A skill approved six months ago may be quietly out of date with current institutional policy.
Is the loop shape still right for this kind of work? Some projects (a one-off report) may not need the full six-stage machinery; forcing it on everything defeats the point.
Keep a short reflection.md per cadence — three or four honest lines is enough. This is the artifact that lets the process itself improve, not just each document that passes through it.

What ties it together: the committed artifact
Across all six stages, the throughline is the same: each stage ends by writing a file, and the next begins by reading it.

For non-coding work, a simple version of the chain looks like:

intent.md      → what's the problem / opportunity, and what does done look like
spec.md        → structure, scope, requirements — locked in before drafting (skip for quick tasks)
draft.md       → the agent-produced content, informed by any relevant skills
review.md      → what was checked, what was flagged, what a human decided
final.*        → the published/submitted artifact (doc, deck, note)
feedback.md    → what happened after — becomes the next intent.md (per document)
reflection.md  → periodic check on the process itself, not any one document
Skills sit alongside this chain rather than inside it — approved once by a domain expert, then reused across many drafts and reviews until the underlying standard changes.

You don't need special tooling for this — a folder per project with these files, or a Cowork session that keeps producing and referencing them, is enough to start.

Getting started (without overbuilding it)
You don't need all six stages formalized on day one. Start small:

Pick one recurring type of document you produce often (a concept note, a policy brief, a course design doc).
Write an intent.md template for it — three or four questions that capture what you're trying to do before you open Cowork.
Decide, honestly, whether it needs a spec step. If getting the scope wrong would cost more than an hour to fix, add it. If not, don't.
Keep one context file with the standing facts, style, and constraints the agent should always know for this kind of work, so you stop re-explaining them.
Turn your first repeated correction into a skill — draft it narrowly, have the relevant expert (possibly you, wearing that hat) approve it before it runs unsupervised.
Add one review checkpoint — a short list of things you always check before something goes out — and ask the agent to run it against every draft.
Log outcomes after something is published, and set a reflection cadence (monthly is plenty at first) to ask whether the process is actually earning its keep.
Once this rhythm feels natural for one document type, extend it to others. The goal isn't process for its own sake — it's making sure your attention goes to the judgment calls, not to work an agent can already do well.

